Here's a modified Java code that can calculate the average age of a list of people and group them into different categories based on their age. It also includes error handling mechanisms to handle cases where the list of people is empty or there are negative age values:
```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import org.json.JSONArray;
import org.json.JSONObject;
public class PeopleAgeCalculator {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String jsonStr = "{\n" +
                "  \"people\": [\n" +
                "    {\n" +
                "      \"name\": \"John\",\n" +
                "      \"age\": 25\n" +
                "    },\n" +
                "    {\n" +
                "      \"name\": \"Mary\",\n" +
                "      \"age\": 30\n" +
                "    },\n" +
                "    {\n" +
                "      \"name\": \"Mike\",\n" +
                "      \"age\": 35\n" +
                "    }\n" +
                "  ]\n" +
                "}";

        JSONObject jsonObj = new JSONObject(jsonStr);
        JSONArray peopleArr = jsonObj.getJSONArray("people");
        List<Integer> ageList = new ArrayList<Integer>();
        int sum = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < peopleArr.length(); i++) {
            JSONObject personObj = peopleArr.getJSONObject(i);
            int age = personObj.getInt("age");

            if (age < 0) {
                System.out.println("Error: Negative age value detected.");
                return;
            }

            ageList.add(age);
            sum += age;
        }

        if (ageList.size() == 0) {
            System.out.println("Error: Empty list of people.");
            return;
        }

        double averageAge = (double) sum / ageList.size();
        System.out.println("Average age: " + averageAge);

        Map<String, List<String>> ageGroupMap = new HashMap<String, List<String>>();

        for (int i = 0; i < ageList.size(); i++) {
            int age = ageList.get(i);
            String ageGroup = getAgeGroup(age);

            if (!ageGroupMap.containsKey(ageGroup)) {
                ageGroupMap.put(ageGroup, new ArrayList<String>());
            }

            JSONObject personObj = peopleArr.getJSONObject(i);
            String name = personObj.getString("name");
            ageGroupMap.get(ageGroup).add(name);
        }

        System.out.println("Age groups:");

        for (Map.Entry<String, List<String>> entry : ageGroupMap.entrySet()) {
            System.out.println(entry.getKey() + ": " + entry.getValue());
        }
    }

    private static String getAgeGroup(int age) {
        if (age < 18) {
            return "Under 18";
        } else if (age < 30) {
            return "18-29";
        } else if (age < 40) {
            return "30-39";
        } else {
            return "40 and over";
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, we first parse the JSON string and extract the list of people. We then loop through the list and calculate the sum of ages and create a list of age values. We also check for negative age values and empty list of people and handle them accordingly.
After calculating the average age, we create a map to group people into different age categories. We define the age categories based on the age values and use the `getAgeGroup` method to determine which category a person belongs to. We then loop through the list of people again and add their names to the corresponding age category in the map.
Finally, we print out the average age and the age groups with their corresponding names.

