Yes, I can help you with that. Here's a C# code that adds the binary values of 101 and 010 together, while taking into account the carryover:
```
string binary1 = "101";
string binary2 = "010";
int carry = 0;
string result = "";
// Loop through each character of the binary values from right to left
for (int i = binary1.Length - 1; i >= 0; i--)
{
    int digit1 = binary1[i] - '0'; // Convert the character to an integer
    int digit2 = binary2[i] - '0'; // Convert the character to an integer
    // Add the digits along with the carryover
    int sum = digit1 + digit2 + carry;
    // Determine the new carryover value
    carry = sum / 2;
    // Determine the new digit value
    int digit = sum % 2;
    // Add the new digit to the result string
    result = digit + result;
}
// If there is still a carryover, add it to the result string
if (carry > 0)
{
    result = carry + result;
}
// Output the result
Console.WriteLine("101 + 010 = " + result);
```
The output of this code is `101 + 010 = 111`, which is the correct binary value for the sum of 101 and 010, taking into account the carryover.

