To model changes in oxygen saturation levels over time, we can use a JavaScript simulation that calculates oxygen saturation levels based on altitude and time. Here's an example code snippet:
```
function simulateOxygenSaturation(altitude, time) {
  const maxOxygenSaturation = 100; // Maximum oxygen saturation level
  let oxygenSaturation = maxOxygenSaturation; // Starting oxygen saturation level

  // Calculate oxygen saturation based on altitude and time
  for (let i = 0; i < time; i++) {
    if (altitude >= 2500) {
      oxygenSaturation -= 2; // Decrease by 2% per hour above 2500m
    } else {
      oxygenSaturation -= 1; // Decrease by 1% per hour below 2500m
    }
  }

  // Ensure oxygen saturation level doesn't go below 0
  if (oxygenSaturation < 0) {
    oxygenSaturation = 0;
  }

  return oxygenSaturation;
}
```
This function takes in the athlete's current altitude and the time spent at that altitude, and calculates their oxygen saturation level over time. We can use this function to simulate the effects of high-altitude training on oxygen utilization in elite athletes.

