How can I create a C# function that searches a given string using regular expressions and returns the index of the first occurrence of a search term and its length, even if the search term contains special characters or white spaces? Here's a sample string and search term in C# code format:
```
string text = "This is a sample text for testing purposes. Let's see if our function can handle special characters and white spaces.";
string searchTerm = @"\b[Tt]esting\s\w+\b";
```
Can you provide me with a function that achieves this using regular expressions in C# code?

output:
Sure, here's a C# function that uses regular expressions to search a given string and returns the index of the first occurrence of the search term and its length:
```csharp
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
public static Tuple<int, int> FindSearchTerm(string text, string searchTerm)
{
    Regex regex = new Regex(searchTerm);
    Match match = regex.Match(text);
    if (match.Success)
    {
        return new Tuple<int, int>(match.Index, match.Length);
    }
    else
    {
        return null;
    }
}
```
You can call this function with your sample string and search term like this:
```csharp
string text = "This is a sample text for testing purposes. Let's see if our function can handle special characters and white spaces.";
string searchTerm = @"\b[Tt]esting\s\w+\b";
Tuple<int, int> result = FindSearchTerm(text, searchTerm);
if (result != null)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Index: " + result.Item1 + ", Length: " + result.Item2);
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Search term not found.");
}
```
This will output: "Index: 29, Length: 14", which is the index and length of the first occurrence of the search term in the sample string.

