To sort the books in chronological order based on their publication date, you can use the `std::sort` function from the `<algorithm>` library in C++. You would need to define a custom comparison function that compares the publication dates of two books and pass it as the third argument to `std::sort`.
Here's an example code snippet:
```c++
#include <algorithm>
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
struct Book {
    std::string title;
    std::string author;
    int year;
};
bool compare_books(const Book& book1, const Book& book2) {
    return book1.year < book2.year;
}
int main() {
    std::vector<Book> books;
    std::ifstream file("books.csv");
    std::string line;
    while (std::getline(file, line)) {
        std::stringstream ss(line);
        std::string title, author, year_str;
        std::getline(ss, title, ',');
        std::getline(ss, author, ',');
        std::getline(ss, year_str);
        int year = std::stoi(year_str);
        books.push_back({title, author, year});
    }
    std::sort(books.begin(), books.end(), compare_books);
    for (const auto& book : books) {
        std::cout << book.title << " by " << book.author << " (" << book.year << ")\n";
    }
    return 0;
}
```
The sorted order of the books would be from the earliest publication date to the latest publication date.

To solve this puzzle, we can use PHP to read the data from the spreadsheet and analyze it to determine the appropriate consequence for each student based on the factors you mentioned. Here is a sample code to accomplish this task:
```
<?php
