There are several data structures that can be used to store the number of times a word appears in a document, each with its own advantages and disadvantages. Here are some commonly used data structures:
1. Array: An array can be used to store the frequency of each word in a document. Each element in the array represents the frequency of a particular word. The advantage of using an array is that it is simple and easy to implement. However, it is not very efficient for large documents, as it requires a lot of memory.
Here's an example code snippet:
```
int[] wordFreq = new int[10000]; // assuming 10000 unique words in the document
// increment the frequency of a word
wordFreq[wordIndex]++; // where wordIndex is the index of the word in the array
```
2. Hash table: A hash table can be used to store the frequency of each word in a document. Each word is mapped to a unique key, and the value associated with the key is the frequency of the word. The advantage of using a hash table is that it is more memory-efficient than an array, as it only stores the words that appear in the document. However, it can be slower than an array for small documents.
Here's an example code snippet:
```
HashMap<String, Integer> wordFreq = new HashMap<>();
// increment the frequency of a word
if (wordFreq.containsKey(word)) {
    wordFreq.put(word, wordFreq.get(word) + 1);
} else {
    wordFreq.put(word, 1);
}
```
3. Trie: A trie can be used to store the frequency of each word in a document. Each node in the trie represents a character in a word, and the frequency of the word is stored at the leaf node. The advantage of using a trie is that it is memory-efficient and can be faster than a hash table for large documents. However, it requires more complex implementation.
Here's an example code snippet:
```
class TrieNode {
    int freq;
    TrieNode[] children;
    public TrieNode() {
        freq = 0;
        children = new TrieNode[26]; // assuming only lowercase letters are used
    }
}
TrieNode root = new TrieNode();
// insert a word into the trie
TrieNode node = root;
for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {
    int index = c - 'a';
    if (node.children[index] == null) {
        node.children[index] = new TrieNode();
    }
    node = node.children[index];
}
node.freq++;
// query the frequency of a word
TrieNode node = root;
for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {
    int index = c - 'a';
    if (node.children[index] == null) {
        return 0;
    }
    node = node.children[index];
}
return node.freq;
```
In terms of optimization, one approach is to use a combination of data structures. For example, a hash table can be used to store the frequency of frequently occurring words, while a trie can be used to store the frequency of less frequently occurring words. This can help balance memory usage and query performance.
