3. Using the dimensions of the USB drive and the foam buffer, create a 3D model of the foam insert in a CAD software. The foam should fit snugly around the USB drive to prevent any movement during shipping and handling. I recommend using a foam density of around 1.7 lbs/ft³ for this application, as it provides good cushioning while still being lightweight.
4. Once you have the foam insert designed, create a negative mold of it in the shape of the clamshell package. This can be done using a CNC machine or 3D printing. The mold should be made of a durable material like aluminum or steel to ensure it can withstand multiple uses.
5. Using the negative mold, create the clamshell package by vacuum forming a thermoplastic sheet over it. I recommend using a material like PETG or PVC for the clamshell, as they are both durable and easy to form.
6. Once the clamshell is formed, trim any excess material and attach the foam insert to the cavity using a strong adhesive like hot melt glue.
7. Finally, add any necessary branding or labeling to the clamshell package to make it stand out on store shelves.
